<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 720 0 R
/E (Experiential Learning Theory. Developed by David A. Kolb; updated by Kolb and Kolb \(2022\). Explains how learning is a process where knowledge is developed through experience, reflection, understanding, and application. In this study, it helps explain how students develop competencies in Forensic Ballistics through theoretical concepts and practical laboratory experiences across four stages: Concrete Experience, Reflective Observation, Abstract Conceptualization, and Active Experimentation.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
229 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 721 0 R
/E (Competency-Based Learning. Supported by Stewart \(2021\) and Wang et al. \(2021\). Emphasizes the development and demonstration of specific knowledge and skills rather than mere completion of lessons or examinations. It helps explain the learning outcomes, instructional strategies, feedback, and assessment practices expected in Forensic Ballistics.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
